is a common mistake when practising emergency?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named plan, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the plan used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"A learner is practising emergency. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Life safety first; smoke/fire are dangerous. uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Life safety first; smoke/fire are dangerous.","Treating drills as jokes slows evacuation.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about emergency?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Treating drills as jokes slows evacuation.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about emergency.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: alarm → line up → walk to assembly → teacher check names.","Treating drills as jokes slows evacuation.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Emergency plans cover fire/lockdown/evacuation signals, assembly points and knowing not to go back for… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Treating drills as jokes slows evacuation.","Map your class emergency route and write three student responsibilities during a drill.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of emergency?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating emergency on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Life safety first; smoke/fire are dangerous. Reasoning: I used emergency by attending to plan rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with emergency, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Treating drills as jokes slows evacuation. Correction: Life safety first; smoke/fire are dangerous. Why: the correction restores accurate use of emergency.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y4-w30-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise emergency.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use emergency with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify plan and explain why it matters.","I can practise evacuate independently on a new prompt.","I can notice contact needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Emergency plans cover fire/lockdown/evacuation signals, assembly points and knowing not to go back for belongings. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Emergency plans cover fire/lockdown/evacuation signals, assembly points and knowing not to go back for belongings."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about emergency?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: alarm → line up → walk to assembly → teacher check names.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy should you not run back into a building for a bag during a fire drill?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLife safety first; smoke/fire are dangerous.","explain":"Emergency plans cover fire/lockdown/evacuation signals, assembly points and knowing not to go back for belongings.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Treating drills as jokes slows evacuation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Emergency plans.","caption":"Drills are serious.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Alarm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">assemble</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">No re-enter</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">for bags</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: alarm → line up → walk to assembly → teacher check names. Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: alarm → line up → walk to assembly → teacher check names. Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy 